INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'cbp-november-recruiting-events-20261030', 'global', 'USA',
'CBP Jobs: November Virtual Recruiting Events and Up to $60,000 in Incentives',
'',
'United States Customs and Border Protection has set five virtual recruiting events for November 2026, starting with the Virtual Career Expo on 3 November 2026; new CBP officers and agents may be eligible for up to $60,000 in incentives at certain locations.',
'',
$$<p>United States Customs and Border Protection has opened registration for five virtual recruiting events in November 2026, giving jobseekers a direct route to recruiters for <strong>CBP jobs</strong> without travelling to a hiring fair. The agency announced the schedule in a release dated 29 October 2026, and the first event, the CBP Virtual Career Expo, takes place on 3 November 2026.</p>
<p>The November programme covers the agency's main hiring lanes in one month: professional and mission-support staff, veterans and military spouses, marine interdiction agents, Border Patrol agents, and Customs and Border Protection officers for northern border assignments. The events are virtual, so candidates can join from anywhere in the United States, and each session is built around speaking to recruiters and to staff who already do the work.</p>
<p>One eligibility limit should be stated at the outset, because it decides who can use this route at all. The agency said federal hiring regulations apply to all CBP positions, and United States citizenship is required. That means these events are aimed at United States citizens, including veterans and military spouses, rather than at overseas applicants looking for visa sponsorship. Readers outside the United States who do not hold United States citizenship will not be eligible for these federal roles, and should treat any agent claiming otherwise with suspicion.</p>
<h2>CBP jobs: the November virtual event schedule</h2>
<p>The agency listed five events. Two session times are confirmed in the agency's own event listings, while the remaining sessions are listed by date, with details on the registration page:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th>Who it is for</th></tr>
<tr><td>3 November 2026</td><td>CBP Virtual Career Expo: We Hire Heroes, 11:00 AM to 2:00 PM Eastern Time</td><td>All career paths, with a focus on veterans and military-connected candidates</td></tr>
<tr><td>10 November 2026</td><td>Veteran and Military Spouse Recruitment Webinar, 12:00 PM to 2:00 PM Eastern Time</td><td>Veterans and military spouses</td></tr>
<tr><td>12 November 2026</td><td>AMO Marine Interdiction Agent Recruitment Webinar</td><td>Marine interdiction agent candidates</td></tr>
<tr><td>17 November 2026</td><td>Border Patrol Agent Recruitment Webinar, 11:00 AM Eastern Time</td><td>Border Patrol agent candidates</td></tr>
<tr><td>18 November 2026</td><td>OFO Northern Border CBP Officer Recruitment Webinar</td><td>CBP officer candidates for northern border assignments</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For the 12 November and 18 November webinars, the release gives the date and the role but not the session time, so candidates should confirm the time on the registration page. Registration for all five events is through join.cbp.gov, and the agency advises registering in advance.</p>
<h2>What the events cover</h2>
<p>According to the release, the virtual events let participants connect with recruiters and learn about career paths across the agency. Participants hear directly from CBP employees serving in these roles, learn about the hiring process, and receive guidance on applications and resumes. That structure matters for federal hiring, where the questionnaire, the resume format, and the eligibility rules can screen out good candidates early.</p>
<p>The 3 November expo is the broadest session. The agency's listing for it promises one-to-one chats with recruiters, booths with information on different career paths, and a webinar on life at CBP. The later webinars are narrower by design: 10 November speaks to veterans and military spouses, 12 November covers Air and Marine Operations marine interdiction agents, 17 November covers Border Patrol agents, and 18 November covers Office of Field Operations officers for the northern border.</p>
<h2>Pay, training and the $60,000 incentive</h2>
<p>The agency said CBP offers training, competitive pay and robust benefits. It also said new CBP officers and agents may be eligible for up to $60,000 in incentive payments at certain locations. That figure is worth reading carefully. It is a maximum, it applies to officers and agents rather than to every role, and it applies at certain locations only, which the agency has not listed in this release. It is not a starting salary, and it is not promised to every recruit.</p>
<p>Federal law enforcement roles also carry background checks, medical and fitness standards, and academy training, and the exact terms depend on the role and duty location. Candidates should ask recruiters at the event about the duty locations that carry incentives, the service commitment attached to any payment, and the full hiring timeline, rather than assuming the maximum figure applies to their application.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>The practical steps, in order, are short. First, visit join.cbp.gov and register in advance for the event that matches the role you want, starting with the 3 November 2026 expo if you are still choosing between roles. Second, prepare a federal-style resume before the session, so a recruiter can advise on a document you can actually show them. Third, join the session ready with questions about eligibility, duty locations, the hiring timeline, and any incentive attached to the location you would accept. Fourth, if you are not ready for a November session, sign up for the CBP Talent Network through the same careers site, so you receive information on later events and can connect with a recruiter.</p>
<p>Readers who have followed other federal hiring events on this site will recognise the pattern. The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/opm-federal-hiring-fair-29-october-20261028">federal hiring fair held on 29 October 2026</a> offered the same kind of direct recruiter contact, and the same preparation rule applied: the candidates who got the most from it were the ones who arrived with a finished resume and specific questions. For a broader picture of working in the United States, including visa routes that do apply to foreign workers in other sectors, see our guide to <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/software-engineer-jobs-in-usa-for-foreigners-h-1b-pay-and-hiring-guide-20261029">software engineer jobs in the USA for foreign workers</a>, which explains where sponsorship is realistic and where it is not.</p>
<h2>A note for readers in Nepal and abroad</h2>
<p>For readers in Nepal, the honest position is that these CBP events are not a route into the United States for most applicants, because United States citizenship is required. Their value is mainly for Nepali-Americans, diaspora members, and families advising a relative who is a United States citizen or veteran. The wider lesson still applies: genuine federal recruitment runs only through official channels, in this case join.cbp.gov and the agency's own careers pages. Nobody can arrange selection for a fee, no agent can bypass the citizenship rule, and no payment to an individual can secure a federal job. Anyone asking for money to arrange a CBP appointment should be treated as a scam and ignored.</p>
<p>Border protection work is demanding and public-facing, and these roles carry real responsibility for trade, travel and border security. The November virtual events are a low-cost way to test whether that work fits, with no travel and no fee. For eligible candidates, registering before 3 November 2026 is the sensible first step.</p>
<p><small>Sources: United States Customs and Border Protection release, "CBP hosts November recruiting events", dated 29 October 2026, via <a href="https://www.einpresswire.com/article/953301080/cbp-hosts-november-recruiting-events">EIN Presswire</a>; event dates and session times as listed by CBP and in the agency's event listings; registration via <a href="https://careers.cbp.gov">join.cbp.gov</a>.</small></p>$$,
